+    /// <summary>
+    /// Polish2 (critic rank 4; PRESENTATION ONLY): a river's banks read as earth and turf, not as a pale rock spillway.
+    /// On the dry samples near water (<see cref="WaterField"/>) up to 20 m above it (fading out from 10 m), the bare rock
+    /// and sand of the splats are moved: wet silt (mud) over the last 1.4 m above the waterline, turf creeping over the
+    /// gentler slopes and over the bank's lip (more of it where the slope is gentler, broken by noise, so the edge of the
+    /// grass is ragged), raw earth on the steep cut between; a rock outcrop stays only where the bank is a true cliff (over
+    /// about 55 degrees) and the noise puts one. The cells and their classes are not touched (movement and cover keep them).
+    /// </summary>
